Funny, with open source I see a lot of people agreeing to work
together on defined projects for a community goal.  But for some
reason, a vocal part of the nethack community is different Respondents
have felt free to characterize me and guess at my attitudes, so I'll
return the favor.

Cooperation is given grudgingly in this group; there's an ideology
that people are all solitary actors, who may or may not deign to
submit a piece of code or pitch in a comment if they feel like it, but
we dare not presume to ask people to actually work together on a team
or to make a little bit of a commitment to a communal effort.  It's
always, "you do it and then if enough people X or Y or Z it will catch
on".  After reading here or engaging some of the regulars, as in this
thread, I'm often left with the distinct impression that, for some of
you, nethack is a private Idaho for practicing a concoction of Randian
egoism and "coding by Libertarian principles" - here because those
ideologies work so badly in the real world.

I'll conclude with the observation that those principles don't work
well here either.  We have a devteam that hasn't produced anything in
3 years.  Few dare criticize, and an effective alternative team never
gets formed or even lobbied for because that's against so many of The
Rules.  We're left with some people actually trying to mollify us that
3 years is within the statistical deviation, so cheer up.

There's a lot of talent and motivation in this group, so this is
pathetic.

Jim


